+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Starting the service
====================

    # rcctl enable rpki_prover
    # rcctl start rpki_prover

rpki-prover runs as _rpki-prover and keeps all of its state, a few GB
of it, in ${LOCALSTATEDIR}/db/rpki-prover. On the first start it downloads
the trust anchor locators of the five RIRs into its tals/ subdirectory,
then fetches and validates the whole RPKI, which takes a few minutes.
It logs to syslog (daemon.info, so /var/log/daemon by default), and its
web UI is at http://127.0.0.1:9999/.

Options
=======

There is no configuration file; everything is a command line option, see
"rpki-prover --help". The defaults in the rc.d script are

    rpki_prover_flags=--http-api-port 9999

and are replaced by whatever is set with rcctl, for example to also serve
RTR to routers:

    # rcctl set rpki_prover flags --http-api-port 9999 \
          --with-rtr --rtr-address 0.0.0.0 --rtr-port 8283

The rc.d script always passes --rpki-root-directory
${LOCALSTATEDIR}/db/rpki-prover; do not repeat it in the flags.

TCP-MD5 for RTR
===============

Routers can be made to sign their RTR connections with TCP-MD5, as for
bgpd(8): the keys are IPsec SAs, one line per router in /etc/ipsec.conf,

    tcpmd5 from <rpki-prover's address> to <router's address> \
        spi 0x1000:0x1001 authkey <hex key>:<hex key>

loaded with "ipsecctl -f /etc/ipsec.conf" (and at boot with
"rcctl enable ipsec"), and --rtr-tcp-md5-ipsec turns TCP-MD5 on for the
RTR server. A router with an SA cannot connect without signing, routers
without one connect as usual.

Memory
======

rpki-prover validates in separate worker processes, each with its own heap
limit (--max-rrdp-fetch-memory, --max-validation-memory and so on). Every
process is also bound by the datasize of the "rpki_prover" login class,
${SYSCONFDIR}/login.conf.d/rpki_prover, which allows 8G. If a worker limit
is raised above that, raise the datasize too.

Worker sandboxing
=================

On Linux the validation workers sandbox themselves with Landlock. That is
not available on OpenBSD, and rpki-prover logs "Worker is not sandboxed" at
start; this is expected.
